      "text": "Given that $x$ is an odd number, we are to find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\).\n\nSince $x$ is odd, let's denote $x=2k+1$ for some integer $k$. We want to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the expression when evaluated with $x=2k+1$.\n\nFirst, let's evaluate the expression with $x=2k+1$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(10(2k+1)+2)(10(2k+1)+6)(5(2k+1)+5) &= (20k+22)(20k+16)(10k+15) \\\\\n&= 2(10k+11) \\cdot 8(10k+2)\\cdot5(2k+3) \\\\\n&= 80(10k+11)(10k+2)(2k+3).\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince $10k+2 = 2(5k+1)$, the term $2(10k+11)(2(5k+1))(2k+3)$ clearly includes factors of $2$ and the odd number $5$. As a product of any three consecutive even integers, $10k+2$ is divisible by $2$, and as a product of any three odd integers, it is divisible by $5$. Thus the largest integer $\\mathbf D$ that always divides the expression is $\\boxed{10}$.",
